Looking at this problem, I need to count quadrilaterals formed by 4 vertices of a regular 30-gon where all four angles are less than 120°.

## Setup

Label vertices 0–29 around the circle. Choose 4 vertices in cyclic order A, B, C, D. Let the arc lengths (in edges) between consecutive vertices be a, b, c, d ≥ 1 with a + b + c + d = 30.

**Inscribed angle formula:** The angle at vertex A subtends arc BCD (length b + c), so:
$$\angle A = (b+c) \cdot \frac{360°}{30} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = (b+c) \cdot 6°$$

Similarly: ∠B = (c+d)·6°, ∠C = (d+a)·6°, ∠D = (a+b)·6°.

Note ∠A + ∠C = (a+b+c+d)·6° = 180° (opposite angles sum to 180°, as expected for cyclic quadrilaterals).

## Constraints

All angles < 120° requires each arc-pair sum ≤ 19:
- a + b ≤ 19, b + c ≤ 19, c + d ≤ 19, d + a ≤ 19

Since (a+b) + (c+d) = 30, the constraints c+d ≤ 19 and d+a ≤ 19 are equivalent to a+b ≥ 11 and b+c ≥ 11. So the conditions reduce to:
$$11 \le a+b \le 19, \quad 11 \le b+c \le 19$$

## Counting valid ordered tuples

Set s = a+b, t = b+c. Then a = s−b, c = t−b, d = 30−s−t+b. The constraints a, c, d ≥ 1 give:
$$\max(1,\; s+t-29) \le b \le \min(s-1,\; t-1)$$

For each (s, t) with s, t ∈ {11,...,19}, the count is:
$$N(s,t) = \min(s-1, t-1) - \max(1, s+t-29) + 1$$

**Case s+t ≤ 30:** N = min(s−1, t−1)
**Case s+t ≥ 31:** N = min(s−1, t−1) − (s+t−29)

Computing the sum S = Σ N(s,t) over all 81 pairs:

| s | Sum over t=11..19 |
|---|---|
| 11 | 9×10 = 90 |
| 12 | 10 + 7×11 + 10 = 97 |
| 13 | 10 + 11 + 5×12 + 11 + 10 = 102 |
| 14 | 10 + 11 + 12 + 3×13 + 12 + 11 + 10 = 105 |
| 15 | 10+11+12+13+14+13+12+11+10 = 106 |
| 16 | 105 (symmetric to s=14) |
| 17 | 102 (symmetric to s=13) |
| 18 | 97 (symmetric to s=12) |
| 19 | 90 (symmetric to s=11) |

The symmetry s ↔ 30−s confirms the pattern. Total:
$$S = 2(90 + 97 + 102 + 105) + 106 = 2 \times 394 + 106 = 894$$

## Converting to quadrilateral count

Each ordered tuple (a,b,c,d) with a designated starting vertex (30 choices) gives a quadrilateral, but each quadrilateral is counted 4 times (once per starting vertex). So:

$$\text{Valid quadrilaterals} = \frac{30 \times 894}{4} = \frac{26820}{4} = \boxed{6705}$$

**Verification:** Total ordered tuples = C(29,3) = 3654; total quadrilaterals = 30×3654/4 = 27405 = C(30,4) ✓. The fraction 6705/27405 ≈ 24.5% is reasonable given the constraint requires each angle in (60°, 120°).

